====================================================================================================
S1. Question 1 -- the OBSERVATIONS are theory-free with respect to a0
====================================================================================================
  b is a Voigt-profile fit parameter: a line width in km/s read off the spectrum. No
  gravitational theory, no acceleration scale, no cosmology enters. The b(N) cutoff is an
  empirical envelope. The IGM temperature people infer FROM the cutoff assumes
  photoionization physics -- but not gravity -- and my test used only the thermal floor,
  never their inferred temperature as a framework input.
  => NO CIRCULARITY. The measured b-values cannot have 'used a local a0'; nothing in a
     Voigt fit knows about one. Carl's first reading is answered: the data is clean.

  BUT NOT CONVENTION-FREE, and this is the part the first version of this script missed.
  Hiss et al.'s b0 is the cutoff evaluated AT a pivot column density N_HI,0 that is itself
  z-DEPENDENT (their eqn 11), so the eight Table 4 rows are not at a common column density:
      z  log N_HI,0(z)  b0 (km/s)
    2.0        12.9743      18.22
    2.2        13.0988      16.89
    2.4        13.2233      18.68
    2.6        13.3478      20.41
    2.8        13.4723      22.67
    3.0        13.5968      22.24
    3.2        13.7213      21.65
    3.4        13.8458      20.80
  pivot climbs 0.872 dex across the sequence. Rudie et al. 2012 use 10^13.6 and Bolton
  et al. 2014 use 10^12.95; Hiss say that ~0.3 dex difference 'will certainly lead to
  inconsistent values of b0'. Cross-paper comparison needs (N_HI/N_HI,0)^(Gamma-1)
  rescaling, and the residual method disagreement is the 3.36 km/s channel carried below.
  [OK] pivot span 0.872 dex reproduced from Hiss eqn 11 -- so b0 is theory-free w.r.t. a0 but NOT pivot-free, and a common-pivot rescaling moves the argmax from z = 2.8 to z = 3.0

