  DIRECTION OF THE CORRECTION, honestly: the declining a0(z) fork -- the one the framework
  prefers on the canonical footing -- is the MILDEST, because a0 was SMALLER in the past so
  x = g/a0 was LARGER and the gas was less deep in the modified regime. That helps the
  framework. The rising and local-density forks make it WORSE. But note the ORDER OF
  MAGNITUDE: after the 2026-07-30 kernel-argument correction the conservative channel runs
  1.4-7.4 sigma across all five forks, so on the softest forks this is NOT an exclusion at
  all, and the corpus's banked '~6-8 sigma, strongly disfavoured' is withdrawn.

====================================================================================================
S5. THE FORK THAT MATTERS MORE THAN a0 -- the x-CONVENTION fork, crossed with the footings
====================================================================================================
  Aguirre, Schaye & Quataert 2001, ApJ 561, 550 (DOI 10.1086/323376; astro-ph/0105184v2)
  is DIRECT PRIOR ART on this test. Their Eq. 8 gives the forest absorber's internal
  Newtonian acceleration a_N ~= 4e-13 cm/s^2 = 4.00e-15 m/s^2 at the MOND-self-consistent
  Jeans length ~11 kpc, GAS ONLY. Their verbatim 'The internal acceleration is then ~= a0/170'
  is the MOND-MODIFIED a = sqrt(a_N a0) = 6.93e-13 m/s^2, NOT a_N/a0 -- on their
  a0 = 1.2e-10 the Newtonian ratio is 1/30000 and the identity is a_N/a0 = (a/a0)^2.
  Their Omega_gas = 0.008-0.009 vs 0.045 is the ISOLATED case only, they call it 'still
  plausible', and their conclusion is that MOND yields a reasonable intergalactic gas density
  for ANY assumed external field -- so it is NOT a forest kill and must not be relayed as one.
  Their forest verdict is soft and EFE-degenerate (title ends in '?'); the kill they assert
  is CLUSTERS. Their Eq. 1 is the bare deep limit a = sqrt(a_N a0), which the framework's own
  nu(y) = sqrt(1+1/y) reproduces EXACTLY, so their forest calculation carries over with a0 as
  the only change -- but their EFE is Bekenstein-Milgrom modified GRAVITY, not modified
  inertia, so it is not prior art on an MI external-field prediction.

  THE FORK: what counts as 'the' acceleration of forest gas is convention-owned. Every row
  below is a genuine Newtonian a_N from that paper's own sizes and mass components, plus this
  corpus's two estimators. Crossed with both a0 footings and propagated to sigma at z = 2.8.
  convention                          a_N (m/s^2)     x can   A can   s_stat   s_cal |     x alt   A alt   s_stat   s_cal
  Aguirre EFE a0/g0=170, 21.1 kpc        2.10e-15  2.24e-05    10.3     153s   25.1s |  1.86e-05    10.8     162s   26.6s
  Aguirre iso MOND, gas, 11 kpc          4.00e-15  4.27e-05     8.7     126s   20.6s |  3.54e-05     9.2     133s   21.8s
  Aguirre EFE a0/g0=25, 40 kpc           7.60e-15  8.12e-05     7.4     102s   16.7s |  6.73e-05     7.8     109s   17.8s
  gas only at CDM size 110 kpc           1.10e-14  1.18e-04     6.8      90s   14.8s |  9.73e-05     7.1      96s   15.7s
  gas+DM at 300 kpc (obs 'size')         2.50e-14  2.67e-04     5.5      68s   11.1s |  2.21e-04     5.8      73s   11.9s
  CDM/Newtonian gas+DM, 110 kpc          6.90e-14  7.37e-04     4.3      47s    7.6s |  6.11e-04     4.5      50s    8.2s
  corpus single-absorber (z=2.8)         7.63e-13  8.16e-03     2.4      16s    2.6s |  6.76e-03     2.5      18s    2.9s
  corpus x_rms total-accel (z=2.8)       5.25e-12  5.61e-02     1.5       5s    0.8s |  4.65e-02     1.6       6s    0.9s
  CONVENTION SPAN: a_N ranges 2500x = 3.4 dex, driving the z = 2.8
  calibration-channel exclusion over 0.8 - 27 sigma.
  FOOTING SPAN: the two a0 footings differ by only 1.21x in x, which moves sigma by
  6% at the softest convention.
  => THE CONVENTION IS THE MAJOR AXIS AND THE a0 FOOTING IS THE MINOR ONE. This is the
  opposite of what this script was written to check, and it is the honest ordering.
  [OK] the x-convention fork spans 3.4 dex -- 42x wider in log than the a0 footing fork -- yet the exclusion's SIGN survives all of it (softest 0.8 sigma on the calibration channel, at 'corpus x_rms total-accel (z=2.8)'); the MAGNITUDE, however, is convention-owned to a factor 32 and must never be quoted bare

====================================================================================================
S6. THE CAVEATS THAT ACTUALLY MATTER, now that the data is sourced
====================================================================================================
  (a) THE b BUDGET IS LCDM-HYDRO-CALIBRATED. The forest b decomposition (thermal + Hubble +
      peculiar) and the f_pec split are calibrated on hydro simulations run with STANDARD
      gravity. In a self-consistent MI universe the IGM's thermal and density structure
      would itself differ. Which way does that cut? Most likely AGAINST the framework:
      amplified diffuse motions would inject extra kinetic energy and raise the IGM
      temperature, widening b further. But that is a plausibility argument, not a
      calculation, and a self-consistent MI hydro simulation does not exist.
      INDEPENDENT SUPPORT FOR THE SIZE OF THIS CAVEAT: Arnold, Puchwein & Springel 2015
      (MNRAS 448, 2275; arXiv:1411.2600) document that the forest is systematics-limited at
      roughly the 10% level for ANY model -- GR itself misses Kim et al. 2007 at z = 3 by
      more than the error bars, attributed to continuum placement and unmodelled low-density
      IGM heating. That is exactly the size of the calibration channel carried here.
  (b) THE CALIBRATION SYSTEMATIC IS REAL AND IS NOT IN TABLE 4. Hiss et al. Sec. 5.3 find
      their b0(z=2.4) = 18.68 +0.74/-1.07 km/s sits >3 sigma above Rudie et al. 2012's
      measurement rescaled to their own pivot (15.32 +/- 0.55 km/s), and attribute it to
      'a different implementation of the cutoff and VP-fitting algorithms' -- least squares
      vs least absolute deviation. 3.36 km/s, ~18% of b0, ~4.6x the median
      statistical bar, and NOT in the published error column. Carried as a second channel.
  (c) THE BRANCH FORK. Whether nu's argument is the LOCAL peculiar field or the
      cosmological/EFE field is not settled by the framework. On the EFE branch
      y = cH(z)/a0 = 21.2-31.9 canonical and nu - 1 is a couple of percent, so
      Arnold et al.'s <10% null transfers a fortiori and the forest constrains nothing.
      Everything in this script tests the LOCAL branch only.
  (d) THE CONVENTION FORK OF S5, which is larger than the a0 fork this script was written
      to close. The sign of the result survives it; the size does not.
  [OK] the caveat that actually drives the numbers is CHECKED, not just recorded: the calibration systematic 3.36 km/s exceeds EVERY Table 4 statistical bar (max 3.12 km/s), which is why the conservative channel is the one to quote, and it exceeds its own 2sigma-rejection variant 2.99 km/s so the headline choice is the conservative one

====================================================================================================
VERDICT
====================================================================================================
  0. THE DATA WAS WRONG AND IS NOW SOURCED. The four cutoff values and the +/- 2.0 km/s bar
     are withdrawn as unsourceable and replaced by Hiss et al. 2018 Table 4 (eight bins,
     real asymmetric errors, plus a separately-carried method systematic). Consequently the
     '7-43 sigma' this script used to close on is withdrawn, not rescaled.
  1. QUESTION 1, ANSWERED NO: the forest measurements cannot have used a local a0. Voigt
     fitting is pure spectroscopy; no acceleration scale enters. The data is clean. But it
     is NOT pivot-free: Hiss's b0 sits at a pivot that climbs 0.87 dex across the sequence,
     which is where the calibration channel comes from.
  2. QUESTION 2, ANSWERED YES -- I OMITTED THE a0(z) FORKS, and that was a real lapse
     against the standing both-ways rule. Now run: five forks, 8 redshifts, two channels.
  3. THE RESULT SURVIVES ALL OF THEM. Weakest exclusion anywhere across the five a0 forks:
     4 sigma statistical, 1.4 sigma against the method systematic.
     The declining canonical a0(z) fork helps the framework most (a0 smaller in the past ->
     x larger -> less deep) but not nearly enough; rising and local-density forks are worse.
  4. BUT THE a0 FORK IS THE WRONG AXIS TO WORRY ABOUT. The two footings differ by 1.21x in x;
     the x-CONVENTION fork inside Aguirre, Schaye & Quataert 2001's own paper spans
     3.4 dex and swings the z = 2.8 calibration-channel exclusion over 0.8-27 sigma. The
     sign is robust; the magnitude is convention-owned. Quote the convention or quote nothing.
  5. THE REAL SOFT SPOTS ARE THE LCDM-CALIBRATED b BUDGET, the method systematic Hiss et al.
     themselves document, and the unclosed EFE-vs-local branch fork -- on the EFE branch the
     framework predicts a few percent, below f(R)'s <10%, and the forest is silent. The
     claim stands as STRONGLY DISFAVOURED ON THE POINTWISE BRANCH pending a self-consistent
     MI hydro calculation. Good question; it did not overturn the result, but it corrected
     the method, and the data audit corrected the number it was applied to.
